A||N||3||SpeedPhreak||my new 84||||5
Z||000000||SpeedPhreak||09-27-1999||09:21 PM||||ok some people read my last post so as u know i just got an 84se.  now there are some quirks that need working out.  <P>1) all of the electrics seem to work slow.  ie, the power windows (sometimes i have to hit the button twice to make it go down) &  the blinkers take a second to start.<P>2) when the heater is on & sometimes there is a whining sound coming from the front passenger area.<P>3) between 60 & 75 the car shakes at varing harshness between the speeds & seems to be worse under exeleration. (steering dampner?)<P>4) sometimes the idle dosnt go down even if i blip the throttle.<P>5) the check engine light comes on after a few hundred feet of high rpm driving (i dont have a code reader or even know how to use one)<P>6) is it supposed to handle this good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>thanx for the help guys.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||DJRice||09-28-1999||09:01 AM||||Well, if the blinkers are slow, then your batt may be low, or your alternator may not be putting out like it should.  If the windows are slow, that just means that they were installed in 1984.  You might try taking the door trim panel off and lubing the moving parts.<P>The vibration could be out of balance tire(s), steering dampener, dry rotted bushings in various places, crappy roads to drive on, etc.<P>As for the whining sound, that could be in the blower motor for AC/Heater, or not.<P>The other prob you would need to pull the code to diagnose,  Its not that big of a deal.  A paper clip, a screwdriver, and a haynes manual should be all you need to do that.   If I had to guess I might say Throttle Position Sensor or Idle Air Control if the computer only sets code when car is at a high idle.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-28-1999||09:03 AM||||noise from the heater motor could be a "simple" as it inhaled a leaf or something. could be a fried motor. you won't know till you get it appart.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||09-28-1999||09:29 AM||||1) Your voltage regulator might be going bad, or maybe your alternator belt is slipping.  The 84's have a voltmeter instead of an oil pressure gauge at the bottom of the tach, right?  What's your voltage reading?  (With a new alternator/voltage reg, my 84 was 14.5 unless the air was on - then it was just under 14)<P>2) Does it whine when you've got air coming out the defroster or dash vents, or only when it blows on your feet?  If it's only when it blows on your feet, I bet the seal on the "flapper" door in the HVAC ducting is bad.  My 86 SE does this, and I just grin and bear it.<P>3) When it comes to wheel vibration, 9 times out of 10 it's a missing wheel weight.<P>4) Ask the Ogre!  (It's probably your idle air control (IAC) valve.)<P>5) To read your ECM trouble codes, take a T-15 Torx screwdriver and remove the small black plastic trim panel in the center console where the cig lighter is. On the passenger side, you'll see the ALDL (a black terminal with 12 or 14 connections).  Go buy a Haynes Fiero manual and read Chapter 6. Then, with the ignition off, bend a paper clip into a U shape, stick one end into terminal A and the other into terminal B (these are usually the bottom two terminals on the passenger side).  Turn the ignition to the "ON" position, but don't start the car.  The "Check Engine" light will flash in a Morse-code-like pattern and tell you what codes have been set - all codes are listed in the Haynes manual.  This is the single best do-it-yourself tip I have ever heard regarding Fieros and has saved me a lot of money.<P>6)  You bet your a$$!||||reg||1
